// Splunk SPL equivalents for the KQL hunting queries.
// Replace the index names with your own. Same logic, same write-ups.
// Hunt: Microsoft agent-framework PyPI compromise - rogue releases
// 1.26.1 and 1.26.2 carrying a credential stealer, published November
// 17, 2026 through Microsoft's legitimate PyPI publisher account (Wiz).
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-18-2026.html
// The .pth filenames are the strongest signal: agent_framework_meta.pth
// (1.26.1) and af_runtime_diag.pth (1.26.2) exist only in the two
// malicious releases, and Python executes .pth files on every
// interpreter start. Variants 3 and 4 are corroboration, not proof on
// their own - webhook.site is a legitimate service and Python reading
// a .env file is normal in development. Tune the thresholds to your
// baseline.

// Variant 1: the malicious .pth persistence files
// Each rogue release drops its own .pth file into the site-packages
// root. A hit means the malicious package was installed on this host:
// treat it as compromised, pin agent-framework==1.26.0, rebuild the
// environment and rotate every secret the machine could reach.
index=endpoint (file_name="agent_framework_meta.pth" OR file_name="af_runtime_diag.pth") earliest=-14d
| stats count as events, values(file_path) as paths, values(process_name) as processes, earliest(_time) as first, latest(_time) as last by host
| sort - events

// Variant 1b: the same .pth files, scoped to site-packages paths
// If Variant 1 is noisy in your environment, restrict to site-packages
// paths - but do not drop Variant 1 entirely: a .pth file with either
// of these names is malicious wherever it sits.
index=endpoint (file_name="agent_framework_meta.pth" OR file_name="af_runtime_diag.pth") file_path="*site-packages*" earliest=-14d
| stats count as events, values(file_path) as paths, values(process_name) as processes, earliest(_time) as first, latest(_time) as last by host
| sort - events

// Variant 2: pip installing the two malicious versions
// The rogue releases were live for about 45 minutes on the evening of
// November 17, 2026 (1.26.1 at 20:35 UTC, 1.26.2 at 21:08 UTC). Any
// install of either version is a compromise indicator. Check the full
// command line on every hit - "1.26.1" would also match a later patch
// release such as 1.26.10 if one ever ships.
index=endpoint (process_name="pip.exe" OR process_name="pip3.exe" OR process_name="python.exe" OR process_name="pythonw.exe") (process="*agent-framework*" OR process="*agent_framework*") (process="*1.26.1*" OR process="*1.26.2*") earliest=-14d
| stats count as events, values(process) as command_lines, values(user) as users, earliest(_time) as first, latest(_time) as last by host
| sort - events

// Variant 2b: any agent-framework install in the November 17 evening
// window - exposure scoping
// The malicious versions were only on PyPI between about 20:35 and
// 21:30 UTC on November 17, so any agent-framework install in that
// window deserves a check even if the version string is not in the
// command line. This is exposure scoping, not a compromise indicator
// on its own: an install of 1.26.0 in the same window is safe, so
// confirm the installed version with pip show agent-framework.
index=endpoint (process_name="pip.exe" OR process_name="pip3.exe" OR process_name="python.exe" OR process_name="pythonw.exe") (process="*agent-framework*" OR process="*agent_framework*") process="*install*" earliest=11/17/2026:20:00:00 latest=11/17/2026:22:00:00
| stats count as events, values(process) as command_lines, values(user) as users, earliest(_time) as first, latest(_time) as last by host
| sort - events

// Variant 3: Python reading credential files
// The stealer reads Azure CLI data, .npmrc, .netrc, .git-credentials,
// Docker configuration and .env files. Python touching one of these is
// normal in development, so this variant is corroboration only: run it
// on hosts that hit Variants 1 or 2, and look for a burst of reads
// across several of these files in the same Python session.
index=endpoint (process_name="python.exe" OR process_name="pythonw.exe") (file_name=".npmrc" OR file_name=".netrc" OR file_name=".git-credentials" OR file_name=".env" OR file_path="*.azure*" OR (file_path="*.docker*" AND file_name="config.json")) earliest=-14d
| stats count as reads, values(file_name) as files, values(file_path) as paths, earliest(_time) as first, latest(_time) as last by host
| sort - reads

// Variant 4: Python talking to webhook.site
// Everything the stealer collects is sent to a webhook.site address.
// webhook.site is a legitimate service, so a hit on its own is not
// proof - but Python reaching it is unusual, and combined with a
// Variant 1 or 2 hit it confirms the exfiltration path. Correlate the
// timestamps: exfiltration follows the first Python start after the
// malicious install.
index=YOUR_NETWORK_INDEX (process_name="python.exe" OR process_name="pythonw.exe") url="*webhook.site*" earliest=-14d
| stats count as connections, values(url) as urls, values(dest_ip) as dest_ips, earliest(_time) as first, latest(_time) as last by src_ip, host
| sort - connections
